    "narrative": "The Austin Waves' offensive engine is centered around **Gunnar Henderson** today, who headlines our lineup with a 81.88 efficiency score. Despite facing an established arm in Buehler, **Gunnar Henderson** benefits from an elite combination of park factors and a prime #2 hole placement, providing the highest ceiling in our rotation. We are also leaning heavily into our speed-focused acquisition strategy; with **Andy Pages** (65.80) and **Jackson Merrill** (68.58) both facing a pitcher with a slow delivery, we anticipate aggressive base-running opportunities. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** remains a high-value play at 70.52; his dynamic platoon advantage against Hancock effectively mitigates the SP_xERA risk, cementing his role as a daily anchor while our primary power threats like **Kyle Tucker** (68.08) look to capitalize on favorable counts.\n\nRegarding our bench decisions, **Miguel Vargas** (43.55) was the final cut, falling just short of the efficiency threshold required to displace **Dominic Smith** or **Wade Meckler**. While **Miguel Vargas** offers solid plate discipline, the optimization model favored the platoon upside provided by **Wade Meckler** against Legumina, whose 3.88 xERA suggests a vulnerability to contact that outweighs the marginal gap in base projections. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, particularly with **Liam Hicks**, who draws a daunting matchup against Skenes (2.99 xERA). While his inclusion is necessary for positional depth, his sub-50 score serves as a reminder to monitor his early-game at-bats closely. Furthermore, as we navigate a roster hampered by the absences of **Luis Robert Jr.**, **Jordan Westburg**, and **Jorge Polanco**, our reliance on high-volume starters like **Nasim Nunez**\u2014despite his diminished 50.99 score\u2014is purely tactical to ensure we remain competitive in the stolen base category, which remains the scarcest commodity for the Austin Waves this season.",
